{"slug": "the-little-book-of-common-sense-investing", "t": "The Little Book of Common Sense Investing", "by": "John C. Bogle", "year": 2007, "area": "money", "unit": "Chapter",
  "verdict": "Read 6 of the 20 chapters. They make the whole case for low-cost index funds; the rest backs it up with more data. Chapters follow the 2017 10th anniversary edition.",
  "units": [
   (R, "1. A Parable", "The Gotrocks family owns every company, then pays helpers to trade among themselves. The whole book in 1 story."),
   (S, "2. Rational Exuberance", "Long-term returns come from what businesses earn and pay out, not from market moods. Skim the numbers."),
   (R, "3. Cast Your Lot with Business", "Own the whole market through an index fund and keep it simple."),
   (R, "4. How Most Investors Turn a Winner's Game into a Loser's Game", "Investors as a group earn the market's return before costs. After costs, the average investor has to trail it."),
   (R, "5. Focus on the Lowest-Cost Funds", "Fees are 1 of the few things you know in advance, and lower costs tend to win."),
   (S, "6. Dividends Are the Investor's (Best?) Friend", "How much of the long-run return comes from dividends, and how fees eat into them."),
   (R, "7. The Grand Illusion", "Fund investors often earn less than their own funds report, because they buy after gains and sell after losses."),
   (S, "8. Taxes Are Costs, Too", "Frequent trading adds taxes. Built around US rules."),
   (S, "9. When the Good Times No Longer Roll", "If future returns are lower, costs eat a bigger share of them."),
   (S, "10. Selecting Long-Term Winners", "How few funds keep beating the market for decades. Skim for the point."),
   (S, "11. \"Reversion to the Mean\"", "Last decade's top funds tend to fall back toward average."),
   (S, "12. Seeking Advice to Select Funds?", "Advisers add a cost of their own. Skim before you hire one."),
   (S, "13. Profit from the Majesty of Simplicity and Parsimony", "Back to the main case: hold the whole market. Mostly repeats chapter 3."),
   (S, "14. Bond Funds", "The same cost math, applied to bonds, where it bites even harder."),
   (S, "15. The Exchange-Traded Fund (ETF)", "ETFs are fine to hold. Trading them often defeats the point."),
   (X, "16. Index Funds That Promise to Beat the Market", "Bogle's doubts about newer index styles. Skip on a first read."),
   (X, "17. What Would Benjamin Graham Have Thought About Indexing?", "Famous investors on index funds. Interesting, not actionable."),
   (R, "18. Asset Allocation I: Stocks and Bonds", "How to split money between stocks and bonds, based on your age and how much risk you can take."),
   (S, "19. Asset Allocation II", "More on building a retirement plan. Built around US accounts."),
   (S, "20. Investment Advice That Meets the Test of Time", "The closing summary of the case. Read it if you want the argument in 1 place.")],
  "ideas": [("Ch. 1", "The Gotrocks parable", "A family owns every company. The more helpers it pays to trade among themselves, the less the family keeps."),
            ("Ch. 4", "Costs come first", "Investors as a group earn the market's return before costs. After costs, the average investor earns less."),
            ("Ch. 5", "Pick the cheaper fund", "Between 2 similar funds, the lower fee is the better bet. You know the fee in advance; you don't know the return."),
            ("Ch. 7", "Don't chase last year's winner", "Buying after gains and selling after losses is how investors earn less than their own funds.")],
  "for": "You want to start investing and don't know what to buy.",
  "skip": "You want a step by step money setup. I Will Teach You to Be Rich covers that."},
